90% (score 390). Structure understood and mostly matching; only register-allocation/scheduling minutiae left.

Semantics (right):
  typedef struct { s32 a,b,c; } V12;              // 12-byte struct, see my project note
  typedef struct { u8 pad[0x94]; s16 f94,f96,f98,f9A; } Obj;
  s32 func_00295490(s32 arg0, s32 arg1, Obj *arg2, V12 *arg3) {
      V12 local; s32 r;
      local = *arg3;
      arg2->f94 = -100; arg2->f96 = -100; arg2->f98 = -100; arg2->f9A = 0;
      r = func_001441B8(0x30, arg1, local.c);          // 2nd arg is arg1 (caller's $a1, still live)
      if (r != 0) func_00294308(r, &local, arg1, arg2, arg0);
      return r;
  }

What differs from the ROM:
1) Register allocation of the 12-byte copy: ROM uses $v1 (ldl/ldr/sdl/sdr) and $a2 (lw 8 -> sw 8),
   i.e. the copy's third word IS the 3rd argument of func_001441B8 - ONE 'lw $a2,0x8($a3)' feeds both
   'sw $a2,0x8($sp)' and the call. Best attempt allocates the copy to $v0/$v1 and re-loads the argument
   from the stack ('lw $a2,0x8($sp)'), or (with arg3->c) emits two separate loads. Writing local = *arg3
   with the argument written as local.c scores best; arg3->c scores worse (470).
2) Because the ROM does not clobber $v0 in the copy, the literal -100 stays live in $v0 from the very
   top of the function (2nd instruction, before the sd saves). In my code the copy takes $v0 so the
   constant is materialised after the copy. Fixing (1) probably fixes (2) automatically.
3) Store order: ROM = 0x98, 0x94, 0x96, then 'sh $zero,0x9a' in the jal delay slot. Mine always puts the
   0x9a zero-store first. Tried several source orders of the four stores - GCC normalises them, no luck.
Tried: field-by-field copy of the struct (much worse, 1565, no ld pair), stores before/after the copy,
a local variable holding -100, packed struct. Keep local = *arg3 + local.c as the argument.

ee-gcc 2.96 -O2 and the address of a local pointer (cost me func_0013AAC0): if you write
  char *tmp;  ... f(a, &tmp); g(b, tmp); f2(a2, &tmp); g2(b2, tmp);
the compiler treats &tmp as reg_equiv_address and rematerializes 'addiu $a2, $sp, off' before
each call, so the pointer never gets a callee-saved register (3 saved regs, no $s2).
Put the buffers and the out-pointer in ONE local struct and pass &l.s:
  struct { char b0[0xA0]; char b1[0xA0]; char *s; } l;
  func_00138758(arg0, arg1[1], &l.s); func_00138900(l.b0, l.s);
  func_00138758(arg0, arg1[2], &l.s); func_00138900(l.b1, l.s);
That compiles to addiu $s2, $sp, 0x140 + move $a2, $s2 before each call: the extra $s2 save.
The idiom is everywhere in 0x138000-0x13C000 (func_00138E00, func_00139230, ...), all of which
keep &tmp in $s2/$s3 across func_00138900. Also: these look-up functions do take two parameters
(a0 is the table passed straight to func_00138758) even though m2c only shows one.

