  p->c = 0x1E; p->data = &D_0044D7F8; p->name = D_00463218; p->a = 0; p->b = 0;
Remaining diff (exactly 2 problems, both ordering): (1) target emits `sh $v0,0xc($a0)` AFTER the address setup, we emit `sw $v1,0x60($a0)` there instead; (2) the target's `sw $v1,0x60($a0)` comes after the `sh`, dead zone.
What is left: GCC insists on scheduling the two pointer stores before the halfword store (it treats 0x60 and 0x0 as the live values and pushes the 0x1E store down). I tried 20+ statement orders (all permutations of the 5 stores), u16/s16/volatile, an s16 local, a pointer-cast version (X3), an inline helper, and a macro. Nothing moved the sh earlier. Suspect the original wrote it as a compound literal / memset, or as an initializer through a different base, or the fields are assigned via a local struct built on the stack (which would explain the dead slot after the const but no stack usage appears in the target, so probably not).

SIF status wait sequence. Source shape is RIGHT and verified (best 1380, 61.6%):
  void func_002D5570(void) {
      s32 t;
      t = func_002DD640();
      *(vu32 *)0x1000F000 = 4;
      <BARRIER>;                 /* sync */
      if (t != 0) func_002DD698();
      while ((*(vu32 *)0x1000F000 & 4) == 0) { }
      t = func_002DD640();
      *(vu32 *)0x1000F000 = 4;
      <BARRIER>;                 /* then tail-calls func_002DD698 if t */
  }
  The '*(vu32 *)0x1000F000 = 4;' interleaves exactly right by itself (lui $v1,0x1000 / addiu $a0,$zero,4 /
  ori $v1,$v1,0xf000 / sw $a0,($v1)), and the wait loop plus the 16-byte loop alignment padding come out
  right too, so once a barrier exists this should drop to 0.
  BLOCKER: the 'sync' instruction (raw ROM word 0x0000000F, verified at 0x002D5590). __asm__ is rejected;
  __sync_synchronize() expands to a libcall 'jal __sync_synchronize' (and that symbol doesn't link).
  Needs an inline-asm barrier or a project macro - see also the note on func_002D5D60.

Same SIF wait pattern as func_002D5570 - BLOCKER is the 'sync' instruction, see that function's notes.
Shape (from m2c + the sibling) should be:
  s64 func_002D5600(void) {
      s32 flag = 0; s64 out;
      func_002D5450(&flag, &out);
      t = func_002DD640();
      *(vu32 *)0x1000F000 = 4;  <BARRIER/sync>
      if (t != 0) func_002DD698();
      while ((*(vu32 *)0x1000F000 & 4) == 0 && flag == 0) { }
      t = func_002DD640();
      *(vu32 *)0x1000F000 = 4;  <BARRIER/sync>
      if (t != 0) func_002DD698();
      return out;   /* out at sp+8, flag at sp+0, frame 0x20 */
  }
  Note the last store to 0x1000F000 is materialised as 'lui $1,0x1001; sw $3,-0x1000($1)' - i.e. gcc folds
  the 0xF000 low half into a negative offset with hi+1, which falls out naturally from the same constant.
  Note also: the local 'flag' must be initialised to 0 BEFORE the func_002D5450 call (it is an out-param).

Best attempt kept: score 190 (94.2%), 34 insns vs 33 target. STRUCT PASSED BY VALUE to func_001441B8 IS CONFIRMED: a 12-byte struct (3 s32 or 3 f32) copied from *arg3 to a stack local with ldl/ldr/lw + sdl/sdr/sw. The struct type determines nothing (s32, u32 and f32 variants all compile identically), so just use 'typedef struct { s32 a,b,c; } Thing;'.
Body: s32 func_0029AF78(s32 arg0, s32 arg1, s32 arg2, Thing *arg3) {
          Thing local; s32 temp; local = *arg3;
          temp = func_001441B8(0x1C, local);
          if (temp != 0) { func_0029B0D0(temp, arg1, arg2, arg0); }
          return temp; }
WHAT IS LEFT - two small things, both regalloc:
(a) The ROM's prologue saves arg0 in $s0, arg1 in $s1, arg2 in $s2 and passes them to func_0029B0D0 as $a0=temp,$a1=arg1,$a2=arg2,$a3=arg0. GCC instead assigns $s0=arg2, $s1=arg1, $s2=arg0 and emits an extra 'move $a1, $sp' (it passes the struct local by address because func_001441B8's prototype lacks the by-value struct). That extra 'move $a1, $sp' is the 1 extra insn, and the shuffled s0/s2 assignments are the regalloc diffs. If the prototype is written as 'extern s32 func_001441B8(s32, Thing);' gcc DOES pass it by value (ldl/ldr/lw/sdl/sdr/sw) - it is only when the caller cannot see a by-value struct prototype that it passes a pointer. So: declare func_001441B8 in the real header with a by-value struct (or as taking the same struct the caller has) and the extra 'move $a1,$sp' disappears; the s0/s2 assignment order is then the only thing left.

